WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.067
In 1848,

2
00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:03.167
Ellen and William Craft

3
00:00:03.167 --> 00:00:04.233
escaped slavery

4
00:00:04.233 --> 00:00:05.833
by traveling openly.

5
00:00:06.033 --> 00:00:07.333
Ellen posed as

6
00:00:07.333 --> 00:00:09.033
a white male slaveholder.

7
00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:10.500
Her husband, William,

8
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:11.467
posed as

9
00:00:11.467 --> 00:00:12.900
her enslaved servant.

10
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.133
But that identity created

11
00:00:15.133 --> 00:00:16.500
a dangerous problem.

12
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:17.867
A gentleman might be asked

13
00:00:17.867 --> 00:00:18.967
to sign his name.

14
00:00:18.967 --> 00:00:20.833
Ellen could not write.

15
00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:23.067
So she bound her right hand,

16
00:00:23.067 --> 00:00:24.433
making it appear injured.

17
00:00:24.433 --> 00:00:26.133
A report published weeks

18
00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:27.400
after their escape

19
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:28.700
describes her avoiding

20
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:29.967
signatures this way.

21
00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:31.767
Their later book

22
00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:32.733
explains more:

23
00:00:32.733 --> 00:00:33.633
bandages hid

24
00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:34.967
her beardless chin,

25
00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:37.000
spectacles obscured her face,

26
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.200
and apparent illness

27
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.767
gave her a reason

28
00:00:39.767 --> 00:00:40.933
to avoid conversation.

29
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:42.500
William helped sustain

30
00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:43.400
the performance,

31
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.200
acting as her attendant.

32
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:46.900
Together, they traveled north

33
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:48.600
by train and steamer.

34
00:00:48.833 --> 00:00:50.033
But before Philadelphia,

35
00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:51.633
they faced a test

36
00:00:51.633 --> 00:00:53.633
the disguise could not answer.

37
00:00:53.833 --> 00:00:55.067
In their account,

38
00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:56.300
officials in Baltimore

39
00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:57.133
demanded assurance

40
00:00:57.133 --> 00:00:58.800
that the supposed master

41
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.033
had the right

42
00:01:00.033 --> 00:01:01.700
to take William along.

43
00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:03.467
Looking like a slaveholder

44
00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:05.467
was not proof of ownership.

45
00:01:05.833 --> 00:01:06.967
William’s account says

46
00:01:06.967 --> 00:01:08.067
a conductor confirmed

47
00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:09.200
they had arrived

48
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.333
on his train.

49
00:01:10.333 --> 00:01:11.767
With departure approaching,

50
00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:13.633
the officer allowed them

51
00:01:13.633 --> 00:01:14.600
through, citing

52
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:16.500
the apparent master’s illness.

53
00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.067
They reached Philadelphia

54
00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:19.400
after four days.

55
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.400
Yet escape did not mean

56
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:22.200
lasting safety.

57
00:01:22.567 --> 00:01:23.433
In 1850,

58
00:01:23.433 --> 00:01:25.133
men came to Boston

59
00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:26.400
to recapture them.

60
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:27.767
The Crafts subsequently

61
00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:29.133
left for England.

62
00:01:29.333 --> 00:01:30.600
Their plan exploited

63
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:31.900
the treatment given

64
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.033
to a supposed white gentleman.

65
00:01:34.033 --> 00:01:35.433
But every accommodation

66
00:01:35.433 --> 00:01:36.800
depended on keeping

67
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:38.667
that false identity intact.

68
00:01:38.867 --> 00:01:40.133
They escaped a system

69
00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:41.700
that treated them as property,

70
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:42.967
by making it appear

71
00:01:42.967 --> 00:01:44.567
that one owned the other.
